1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,830
Manila, the ninth of January,

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:04,330
1946.

3
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:06,448
The ballroom of the old High

4
00:00:06,467 --> 00:00:08,212
Commissioner's Residence has been turned

5
00:00:08,232 --> 00:00:09,271
into a courtroom.

6
00:00:09,291 --> 00:00:12,094
Five generals sit at a long table under

7
00:00:12,114 --> 00:00:12,800
the flags.

8
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:15,976
In front of them, a master sergeant of the

9
00:00:15,996 --> 00:00:18,447
United States Army stands beside a large

10
00:00:18,467 --> 00:00:20,211
chart of the Bataan peninsula,

11
00:00:20,231 --> 00:00:21,290
holding a pointer.

12
00:00:22,675 --> 00:00:25,995
His name is James Baldassarre.

13
00:00:26,015 --> 00:00:28,282
He has served twenty-eight years in the

14
00:00:28,302 --> 00:00:30,568
Army, twenty-one of them in the Philippines.

15
00:00:30,588 --> 00:00:32,855
Four years earlier he walked the road

16
00:00:32,875 --> 00:00:35,795
drawn on that chart as a prisoner of war.

17
00:00:35,815 --> 00:00:38,735
The prosecutor asks him what he had to eat

18
00:00:38,755 --> 00:00:42,435
and drink between Mariveles and Balanga.

19
00:00:42,455 --> 00:00:44,375
"No, sir," he answers.

20
00:00:44,395 --> 00:00:46,335
"No food, no water."

21
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:49,960
He describes the bodies he passed between

22
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:52,420
Balanga and Lubao.

23
00:00:52,440 --> 00:00:54,292
"Many of them," he says.

24
00:00:54,312 --> 00:00:55,415
"Hundreds of them.

25
00:00:55,435 --> 00:00:58,784
God knows how many of them." Then he is

26
00:00:58,804 --> 00:01:01,405
asked whether he saw any high Japanese

27
00:01:01,425 --> 00:01:02,902
officers on that road.

28
00:01:02,922 --> 00:01:04,420
And the sergeant turns.

29
00:01:05,775 --> 00:01:08,295
"There is one right in this place now,

30
00:01:08,315 --> 00:01:10,518
right now," he says, "that I can

31
00:01:10,538 --> 00:01:12,740
recognize, who was riding in an official

32
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:14,010
car." He names him.

33
00:01:14,030 --> 00:01:16,375
Lieutenant General Masaharu Homma,

34
00:01:16,395 --> 00:01:18,160
the commander who took Bataan.

35
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:20,659
A reporter for the Associated Press wrote

36
00:01:20,679 --> 00:01:23,158
that every face in the courtroom turned

37
00:01:23,178 --> 00:01:24,550
toward Homma.

38
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:27,230
This is the documented story of the trial

39
00:01:27,250 --> 00:01:28,570
of General Homma:

40
00:01:28,590 --> 00:01:30,525
the men who survived the Bataan Death

41
00:01:30,545 --> 00:01:32,760
March and came back to face the general

42
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:34,715
whose army marched them, the five generals

43
00:01:34,735 --> 00:01:36,670
who judged him, the young lawyers who

44
00:01:36,690 --> 00:01:38,905
defended him, the wife who stood up for

45
00:01:38,925 --> 00:01:41,139
him, a Supreme Court justice who called it

46
00:01:41,159 --> 00:01:42,536
the wrong kind of justice,

47
00:01:42,556 --> 00:01:43,653
and General Douglas MacArthur,

48
00:01:43,673 --> 00:01:45,070
who had the last word.

49
00:01:46,425 --> 00:01:49,193
Most of what you will hear comes from the

50
00:01:49,213 --> 00:01:51,052
trial record itself, more than three

51
00:01:51,072 --> 00:01:52,911
thousand pages typed in that ballroom,

52
00:01:52,931 --> 00:01:55,390
and from the reporters who sat in it.

53
00:01:55,410 --> 00:01:56,629
Where the sources disagree,

54
00:01:56,649 --> 00:01:57,868
we will tell you.

55
00:01:57,888 --> 00:02:00,656
And where a scene rests on one man's diary

56
00:02:00,676 --> 00:02:02,845
or memoir, we will tell you whose.

57
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:07,759
Masaharu Homma was not the kind of

58
00:02:07,779 --> 00:02:09,778
Japanese general American newspapers liked

59
00:02:09,798 --> 00:02:10,585
to draw.

60
00:02:10,605 --> 00:02:15,520
He was born on Sado Island in 1887.

61
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:18,536
As a young officer in the First World War

62
00:02:18,556 --> 00:02:20,882
he was attached to the British Army,

63
00:02:20,902 --> 00:02:23,899
and he later served as military attaché in London.

64
00:02:23,919 --> 00:02:24,904
He wrote poetry.

65
00:02:24,924 --> 00:02:27,593
His wife said he read Galsworthy and

66
00:02:27,613 --> 00:02:28,413
Bernard Shaw.

67
00:02:29,675 --> 00:02:33,075
In December 1941 he commanded Japan's

68
00:02:33,095 --> 00:02:33,928
Fourteenth Army.

69
00:02:33,948 --> 00:02:36,488
On the twenty-second of December his

70
00:02:36,508 --> 00:02:39,969
troops came ashore at Lingayen Gulf on Luzon.

71
00:02:39,989 --> 00:02:41,858
Within weeks Manila had fallen,

72
00:02:41,878 --> 00:02:44,503
and the American and Filipino army under

73
00:02:44,523 --> 00:02:47,147
General MacArthur had pulled back into the

74
00:02:47,167 --> 00:02:49,792
Bataan peninsula, a finger of jungle and

75
00:02:49,812 --> 00:02:52,835
mountains on the western side of Manila Bay.

76
00:02:54,150 --> 00:02:56,694
They held Bataan for three months,

77
00:02:56,714 --> 00:02:59,686
on half rations and then on less.

78
00:02:59,706 --> 00:03:02,678
Malaria and dysentery spread through the lines.

79
00:03:02,698 --> 00:03:05,242
In March, on President Roosevelt's orders,

80
00:03:05,262 --> 00:03:07,807
MacArthur left for Australia and promised

81
00:03:07,827 --> 00:03:08,661
to return.

82
00:03:08,681 --> 00:03:11,653
The men he left behind called themselves

83
00:03:11,673 --> 00:03:13,810
the battling bastards of Bataan.

84
00:03:14,975 --> 00:03:18,753
By early April the army on Bataan was finished.

85
00:03:18,773 --> 00:03:21,191
Major Achille Tisdelle, aide to the

86
00:03:21,211 --> 00:03:23,438
commander there, would later testify that

87
00:03:23,458 --> 00:03:26,058
about forty-three per cent of the troops

88
00:03:26,078 --> 00:03:28,305
were sick with malaria, dysentery or

89
00:03:28,325 --> 00:03:29,053
simple exhaustion.

90
00:03:29,073 --> 00:03:31,674
The men had been on quarter rations.

91
00:03:31,694 --> 00:03:34,315
There were two days of food left.

92
00:03:35,975 --> 00:03:38,702
The commander on Bataan was Major General

93
00:03:38,722 --> 00:03:39,487
Edward King.

94
00:03:39,508 --> 00:03:42,235
Before dawn on the ninth of April,

95
00:03:42,255 --> 00:03:46,192
1942, he sent officers forward under a

96
00:03:46,212 --> 00:03:46,984
white flag.

97
00:03:47,004 --> 00:03:49,360
Later that morning he followed them

98
00:03:49,380 --> 00:03:51,737
himself, in his last clean uniform,

99
00:03:51,757 --> 00:03:54,114
according to the Army's official history.

100
00:03:54,134 --> 00:03:56,490
Japanese planes strafed his car anyway.

101
00:03:56,510 --> 00:04:00,075
It took two hours to travel about three miles.

102
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:03,680
Near the village of Lamao he met Colonel

103
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:06,571
Motoo Nakayama, Homma's senior operations

104
00:04:06,591 --> 00:04:09,200
officer, who arrived in a shiny Cadillac.

105
00:04:09,220 --> 00:04:13,191
Nakayama's first question was whether King

106
00:04:13,211 --> 00:04:14,433
was General Wainwright.

107
00:04:14,453 --> 00:04:15,676
He was not.

108
00:04:15,696 --> 00:04:18,575
Wainwright commanded all the forces in the

109
00:04:18,595 --> 00:04:21,060
Philippines from the island fortress of

110
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:24,040
Corregidor, and Nakayama wanted all of them.

111
00:04:25,275 --> 00:04:27,660
King had come to surrender Bataan only.

112
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:30,065
He had left his sword in Manila,

113
00:04:30,085 --> 00:04:32,814
so he laid his pistol on the table.

114
00:04:32,834 --> 00:04:35,563
He asked again and again what would happen

115
00:04:35,583 --> 00:04:36,593
to his men.

116
00:04:36,613 --> 00:04:39,115
In a sworn statement read at Homma's

117
00:04:39,135 --> 00:04:41,380
trial, King said the Japanese officer

118
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:43,644
finally told him, through the interpreter:

119
00:04:43,664 --> 00:04:45,909
"The Imperial Japanese Army are not

120
00:04:45,929 --> 00:04:47,418
barbarians." With that assurance,

121
00:04:47,438 --> 00:04:50,060
King said, he had to content himself,

122
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:50,880
and surrender.

123
00:04:52,225 --> 00:04:54,951
At the table, Major Tisdelle reached for a

124
00:04:54,971 --> 00:04:55,294
cigarette.

125
00:04:55,314 --> 00:04:58,373
A Japanese aide knocked it out of his hand

126
00:04:58,393 --> 00:05:00,768
and told him to sit at attention.

127
00:05:00,788 --> 00:05:03,163
King and his staff, the Army's historian

128
00:05:03,183 --> 00:05:05,216
later wrote, were in fact hostages,

129
00:05:05,236 --> 00:05:06,605
not prisoners of war.

130
00:05:07,875 --> 00:05:10,319
General King knew how history would

131
00:05:10,339 --> 00:05:11,141
remember him:

132
00:05:11,161 --> 00:05:14,015
as the man who surrendered the largest

133
00:05:14,035 --> 00:05:16,890
force the United States had ever lost.

134
00:05:16,910 --> 00:05:19,765
The Army's official history puts it at

135
00:05:19,785 --> 00:05:20,997
seventy-eight thousand men.

136
00:05:21,017 --> 00:05:23,872
Roughly twelve thousand of them were Americans.

137
00:05:23,892 --> 00:05:25,535
The rest were Filipinos.

138
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:29,679
The Japanese plan for prisoners had been

139
00:05:29,699 --> 00:05:32,420
written in March, for about forty thousand men.

140
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:36,140
Homma's staff officer Toshimitsu Takatsu

141
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:38,309
later testified that they expected thirty

142
00:05:38,329 --> 00:05:40,478
thousand, and were surprised when more

143
00:05:40,498 --> 00:05:41,924
than sixty thousand appeared.

144
00:05:41,944 --> 00:05:45,177
Most of them were sick, and all of them

145
00:05:45,197 --> 00:05:45,997
were hungry.

146
00:05:47,275 --> 00:05:50,090
The plan itself looked simple on paper.

147
00:05:50,110 --> 00:05:53,092
The prisoners would gather at Balanga and

148
00:05:53,112 --> 00:05:54,977
walk north to San Fernando.

149
00:05:54,997 --> 00:05:59,135
From there boxcars would carry them to Capas.

150
00:05:59,155 --> 00:06:02,375
Then a last walk would bring them to a

151
00:06:02,395 --> 00:06:04,535
Filipino army post called Camp O'Donnell.

152
00:06:04,555 --> 00:06:07,158
From Mariveles, at the tip of the

153
00:06:07,178 --> 00:06:08,408
peninsula, to San Fernando,

154
00:06:08,428 --> 00:06:10,615
it was about sixty-five miles on foot.

155
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:13,999
What it was like on the road,

156
00:06:14,019 --> 00:06:16,723
the trial record tells in the words of the

157
00:06:16,743 --> 00:06:17,934
men who walked it.

158
00:06:17,954 --> 00:06:20,053
Colonel John Ball, a regular officer with

159
00:06:20,073 --> 00:06:22,777
thirty years in the Army, was kicked by a

160
00:06:22,797 --> 00:06:24,290
guard and lost his helmet.

161
00:06:24,310 --> 00:06:26,409
He marched bareheaded under the April sun

162
00:06:26,429 --> 00:06:28,725
all the way to Orani.

163
00:06:29,425 --> 00:06:32,458
Ball saw a Filipino soldier dive toward a

164
00:06:32,478 --> 00:06:33,602
ditch for water.

165
00:06:33,622 --> 00:06:35,129
A sentry bayoneted him.

166
00:06:35,149 --> 00:06:37,800
"This Filipino made a noise," Ball told

167
00:06:37,820 --> 00:06:38,563
the court.

168
00:06:38,583 --> 00:06:41,997
"That is one of the things you can't forget.

169
00:06:42,017 --> 00:06:45,049
Made a noise like a baby crying." The

170
00:06:45,069 --> 00:06:47,339
Associated Press reported that the colonel

171
00:06:47,359 --> 00:06:48,885
wept on the stand.

172
00:06:50,225 --> 00:06:51,694
Lieutenant Colonel Willard Waterous,

173
00:06:51,714 --> 00:06:54,299
an Army doctor, testified that about two

174
00:06:54,319 --> 00:06:56,533
thousand Filipino patients from a Bataan

175
00:06:56,553 --> 00:06:58,766
hospital were driven onto the road,

176
00:06:58,786 --> 00:07:01,744
among them men whose legs had only just

177
00:07:01,764 --> 00:07:02,488
been amputated.

178
00:07:02,508 --> 00:07:04,349
Technical Sergeant Horace Clark described

179
00:07:04,369 --> 00:07:07,327
an American who collapsed by the road and

180
00:07:07,347 --> 00:07:10,325
was beheaded with two strokes of a sword.

181
00:07:11,650 --> 00:07:14,913
The Filipino officers had it worst of all.

182
00:07:14,933 --> 00:07:17,787
Captain Pedro Felix was one of several

183
00:07:17,807 --> 00:07:19,839
hundred Filipino officers and sergeants

184
00:07:19,859 --> 00:07:22,124
marched to the Pantingan River.

185
00:07:22,144 --> 00:07:25,026
The guards put cigarettes in their mouths

186
00:07:25,046 --> 00:07:26,269
and lit them.

187
00:07:26,289 --> 00:07:29,171
Then, on a signal, they began bayoneting

188
00:07:29,191 --> 00:07:30,414
and beheading them.

189
00:07:30,434 --> 00:07:32,487
"I was bayoneted four times,

190
00:07:32,507 --> 00:07:33,750
sir," Felix said.

191
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:36,783
His countryman Major Eduardo Vargas

192
00:07:36,803 --> 00:07:38,585
survived the same killing ground.

193
00:07:38,605 --> 00:07:40,388
He lay among the bodies.

194
00:07:40,408 --> 00:07:42,912
"I didn't move," he told the court,

195
00:07:42,932 --> 00:07:45,075
"and even tried not to breathe,

196
00:07:45,095 --> 00:07:47,959
so that they wouldn't notice that I was

197
00:07:47,979 --> 00:07:48,779
alive."

198
00:07:49,675 --> 00:07:52,032
Three years after Bataan, an American Army

199
00:07:52,052 --> 00:07:54,749
film crew met one of the march survivors

200
00:07:54,769 --> 00:07:57,126
in a prison camp in northern Japan,

201
00:07:57,146 --> 00:07:59,163
at the end of the war.

202
00:07:59,183 --> 00:08:01,540
He was a chaplain from Seattle named

203
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:02,220
Leslie Zimmerman.

204
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:04,257
Listen to how he described it,

205
00:08:04,277 --> 00:08:07,675
in his own voice, in 1945.

206
00:08:08,375 --> 00:08:10,616
Well, after the death march into Bataan,

207
00:08:10,636 --> 00:08:12,555
we could stand on almost anything.

208
00:08:12,575 --> 00:08:15,785
We have seen men die and not turn a hair.

209
00:08:15,805 --> 00:08:18,369
We have seen men beaten to death or

210
00:08:18,389 --> 00:08:20,308
brutally treated, and we don't think

211
00:08:20,328 --> 00:08:21,277
anything about it.

212
00:08:21,297 --> 00:08:23,235
We got rather numb to it.

213
00:08:25,075 --> 00:08:26,986
The march ended at Camp O'Donnell,

214
00:08:27,006 --> 00:08:28,595
and the dying went on.

215
00:08:28,615 --> 00:08:32,135
Takatsu told the court that in late May

216
00:08:32,155 --> 00:08:34,455
about three hundred people a day were

217
00:08:34,475 --> 00:08:35,118
dying there.

218
00:08:35,138 --> 00:08:38,078
An Army report written in 1945 counted

219
00:08:38,098 --> 00:08:40,292
about fifteen hundred Americans and

220
00:08:40,312 --> 00:08:42,949
twenty-two thousand Filipinos dead at O'Donnell.

221
00:08:42,969 --> 00:08:44,721
Men collapsed from exhaustion,

222
00:08:44,741 --> 00:08:47,821
the report said, and were sometimes buried

223
00:08:47,841 --> 00:08:50,055
before they were actually dead.

224
00:08:51,350 --> 00:08:53,836
How many died on the march itself,

225
00:08:53,856 --> 00:08:54,911
no one counted.

226
00:08:54,931 --> 00:08:57,059
Historians' estimates today run from about

227
00:08:57,079 --> 00:08:59,924
five thousand to more than ten thousand men.

228
00:08:59,944 --> 00:09:02,788
At the trial the prosecution put the toll

229
00:09:02,808 --> 00:09:05,653
of the march and the first camp together

230
00:09:05,673 --> 00:09:06,727
at seventeen thousand.

231
00:09:06,747 --> 00:09:09,233
You will hear many numbers for Bataan.

232
00:09:09,253 --> 00:09:12,814
None of them is exact, and that is part of

233
00:09:12,834 --> 00:09:13,634
the crime.

234
00:09:14,850 --> 00:09:16,790
And where was the commanding general?

235
00:09:16,810 --> 00:09:19,950
Homma's headquarters was at Balanga,

236
00:09:19,970 --> 00:09:21,550
on the route of the march.

237
00:09:21,570 --> 00:09:25,435
Takatsu put it about five hundred meters

238
00:09:25,455 --> 00:09:26,608
from the road.

239
00:09:26,628 --> 00:09:30,230
In August 1942 Homma was relieved of his

240
00:09:30,250 --> 00:09:31,990
command and called home to Japan.

241
00:09:33,325 --> 00:09:36,085
For almost two years Americans at home

242
00:09:36,105 --> 00:09:37,674
were told almost nothing.

243
00:09:37,694 --> 00:09:41,299
Only in January 1944, after a few

244
00:09:41,319 --> 00:09:43,486
prisoners escaped, did the War Department

245
00:09:43,506 --> 00:09:44,580
release their accounts.

246
00:09:44,600 --> 00:09:47,131
That was when the words Death March

247
00:09:47,151 --> 00:09:48,245
entered American newspapers.

248
00:09:50,075 --> 00:09:52,505
MacArthur came back to the Philippines in

249
00:09:52,525 --> 00:09:55,275
October 1944.

250
00:09:55,295 --> 00:09:59,035
Manila was retaken in early 1945.

251
00:09:59,055 --> 00:10:01,435
In August Japan surrendered.

252
00:10:01,455 --> 00:10:04,695
In September, Homma was arrested in Japan.

253
00:10:06,050 --> 00:10:08,990
On the fifth of December, 1945,

254
00:10:09,010 --> 00:10:11,555
a directive set up a military commission

255
00:10:11,575 --> 00:10:12,655
to try him.

256
00:10:12,675 --> 00:10:15,586
On the twelfth he was flown to Manila.

257
00:10:15,606 --> 00:10:17,418
Five generals were appointed by

258
00:10:17,438 --> 00:10:19,617
MacArthur's command to hear the case:

259
00:10:19,637 --> 00:10:21,151
Major General Leo Donovan,

260
00:10:21,171 --> 00:10:23,358
who presided, Brigadier Generals Robert

261
00:10:23,378 --> 00:10:26,006
Gard, Arthur Trudeau and Warren McNaught,

262
00:10:26,026 --> 00:10:29,250
and Major General Basilio Valdes of the

263
00:10:29,270 --> 00:10:30,070
Philippine Army.

264
00:10:31,225 --> 00:10:34,551
The charge was one charge with forty-eight

265
00:10:34,571 --> 00:10:35,029
specifications.

266
00:10:35,049 --> 00:10:38,165
It said Homma had failed to control the

267
00:10:38,185 --> 00:10:40,740
men under his command and permitted them

268
00:10:40,760 --> 00:10:42,212
to commit brutal atrocities.

269
00:10:42,232 --> 00:10:44,787
Nobody claimed he had personally killed anyone.

270
00:10:44,807 --> 00:10:47,362
The question in front of the commission

271
00:10:47,382 --> 00:10:48,834
was a commander's question:

272
00:10:48,854 --> 00:10:52,165
what a general owes for what his army does.

273
00:10:53,475 --> 00:10:57,512
His defense was given to six young Army lawyers.

274
00:10:57,532 --> 00:11:00,218
The chief was Major John Skeen,

275
00:11:00,238 --> 00:11:02,021
a twenty-seven-year-old admiralty lawyer

276
00:11:02,041 --> 00:11:02,922
from Baltimore.

277
00:11:02,942 --> 00:11:06,364
According to a 1996 history of the trial,

278
00:11:06,384 --> 00:11:09,222
Skeen had never argued a case in court.

279
00:11:09,242 --> 00:11:11,723
One of his team, Lieutenant Robert Pelz,

280
00:11:11,743 --> 00:11:12,815
kept a diary.

281
00:11:14,175 --> 00:11:16,095
Pelz wrote that at their first meeting

282
00:11:16,115 --> 00:11:18,485
Homma looked like a tired-out grandfather

283
00:11:18,505 --> 00:11:21,780
who had girded his loins for a last battle.

284
00:11:21,800 --> 00:11:24,342
The lawyers had fifteen days between the

285
00:11:24,362 --> 00:11:25,806
arraignment and the trial.

286
00:11:25,826 --> 00:11:28,368
Skeen swore that eighteen days had not

287
00:11:28,388 --> 00:11:30,931
been enough to investigate even a small

288
00:11:30,951 --> 00:11:32,415
part of forty-eight specifications.

289
00:11:34,050 --> 00:11:36,475
The defense also objected to something else.

290
00:11:36,495 --> 00:11:38,921
The man who had ordered the trial,

291
00:11:38,941 --> 00:11:41,017
whose command had chosen the court,

292
00:11:41,037 --> 00:11:43,462
and who would review its sentence was

293
00:11:43,482 --> 00:11:45,742
MacArthur, the general Homma had driven

294
00:11:45,762 --> 00:11:46,574
off Luzon.

295
00:11:46,594 --> 00:11:49,902
Their motion said no one man should be

296
00:11:49,922 --> 00:11:51,150
accuser, prosecutor, judge,

297
00:11:51,170 --> 00:11:53,646
jury and court of final appeal.

298
00:11:53,666 --> 00:11:55,330
The trial went ahead.

299
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,200
The trial opened on the third of January,

300
00:11:59,220 --> 00:12:01,700
1946.

301
00:12:01,720 --> 00:12:03,900
Its first witness was a Japanese general,

302
00:12:03,920 --> 00:12:07,277
Takatsu, Homma's own former staff officer.

303
00:12:07,297 --> 00:12:10,464
He told the court he was honor bound not

304
00:12:10,484 --> 00:12:13,651
to speak badly of his old commander in his

305
00:12:13,671 --> 00:12:14,005
presence.

306
00:12:14,025 --> 00:12:15,776
He then described the plan,

307
00:12:15,796 --> 00:12:18,275
the numbers, and the deaths at O'Donnell.

308
00:12:18,975 --> 00:12:21,667
Over the next three weeks the prosecution

309
00:12:21,687 --> 00:12:23,603
called one hundred and thirty-six

310
00:12:23,623 --> 00:12:26,315
witnesses and put in more than three

311
00:12:26,335 --> 00:12:29,027
hundred exhibits, most of them sworn statements.

312
00:12:29,047 --> 00:12:30,963
Americans and Filipinos, soldiers and

313
00:12:30,983 --> 00:12:33,695
civilians, came to the ballroom to testify.

314
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:36,920
The historian Lawrence Taylor later noted

315
00:12:36,940 --> 00:12:39,899
that not one of them tied Homma himself to

316
00:12:39,919 --> 00:12:40,977
any particular atrocity.

317
00:12:40,997 --> 00:12:43,494
That was not what the prosecution was

318
00:12:43,514 --> 00:12:44,572
trying to prove.

319
00:12:44,592 --> 00:12:47,807
Its case was that a commander who let such

320
00:12:47,827 --> 00:12:49,604
things happen on that scale,

321
00:12:49,624 --> 00:12:52,500
on his own road, was responsible for them.

322
00:12:54,125 --> 00:12:56,285
On the ninth of January came Sergeant

323
00:12:56,305 --> 00:12:58,289
Baldassarre and his pointer,

324
00:12:58,309 --> 00:13:01,233
and the moment he turned toward the defendant.

325
00:13:01,253 --> 00:13:04,253
The reporters wrote that Homma grinned nervously.

326
00:13:04,273 --> 00:13:07,897
Outside the courtroom, Baldassarre told a

327
00:13:07,917 --> 00:13:10,585
reporter what he thought of the whole proceeding.

328
00:13:10,605 --> 00:13:12,937
They should never give him a trial,

329
00:13:12,957 --> 00:13:13,609
he said.

330
00:13:13,629 --> 00:13:15,645
He never gave us no trial.

331
00:13:16,925 --> 00:13:19,549
The next day it was Captain Felix.

332
00:13:19,569 --> 00:13:22,194
The presiding general asked him to take

333
00:13:22,214 --> 00:13:23,327
off his shirt.

334
00:13:23,347 --> 00:13:25,972
He stood, turned, and showed the five

335
00:13:25,992 --> 00:13:28,994
generals his back and the scars of four

336
00:13:29,014 --> 00:13:29,749
bayonet wounds.

337
00:13:29,769 --> 00:13:31,638
The Associated Press reported that

338
00:13:31,658 --> 00:13:32,394
spectators gasped.

339
00:13:32,414 --> 00:13:35,794
On the same day Colonel Ball wept as he

340
00:13:35,814 --> 00:13:38,400
described the road to Orani.

341
00:13:39,100 --> 00:13:41,732
On the fourteenth of January a Japanese

342
00:13:41,752 --> 00:13:45,142
woman in a kimono arrived by plane from Tokyo.

343
00:13:45,162 --> 00:13:48,240
She was Fujiko Homma, the general's wife.

344
00:13:49,550 --> 00:13:51,870
On the twenty-first, the last two

345
00:13:51,890 --> 00:13:53,820
prosecution witnesses took the stand.

346
00:13:53,840 --> 00:13:56,519
One was Major Tisdelle, General King's

347
00:13:56,539 --> 00:13:59,810
aide, who had seen the surrender at Lamao.

348
00:13:59,830 --> 00:14:01,690
The other was Colonel John Pugh,

349
00:14:01,710 --> 00:14:03,910
General Wainwright's aide on Corregidor.

350
00:14:03,930 --> 00:14:06,621
Pugh was shown a captured Japanese

351
00:14:06,641 --> 00:14:08,698
photograph of Wainwright sitting across a

352
00:14:08,718 --> 00:14:11,050
table from Homma on the sixth of May,

353
00:14:11,070 --> 00:14:12,950
1942.

354
00:14:13,650 --> 00:14:16,439
General Homma, Pugh said, was the most

355
00:14:16,459 --> 00:14:18,124
prominent figure in the picture.

356
00:14:18,144 --> 00:14:20,910
Of those last days on Corregidor he said:

357
00:14:20,930 --> 00:14:23,673
"Corregidor at that time was a nightmare

358
00:14:23,693 --> 00:14:25,970
which I have tried to forget for four

359
00:14:25,990 --> 00:14:28,267
years, which I now realize I shall never

360
00:14:28,287 --> 00:14:30,010
forget." With that, the prosecution rested.

361
00:14:31,725 --> 00:14:34,545
The defense called Japanese officers first.

362
00:14:34,565 --> 00:14:38,245
Homma's chief of staff, Lieutenant General

363
00:14:38,265 --> 00:14:41,305
Takeji Wachi, testified that there had

364
00:14:41,325 --> 00:14:43,825
been no cases of willful atrocities.

365
00:14:43,845 --> 00:14:46,758
A Japanese censor called Homma more of a

366
00:14:46,778 --> 00:14:48,445
desk general than a field commander.

367
00:14:49,850 --> 00:14:51,310
Then, on the fifth of February,

368
00:14:51,330 --> 00:14:53,212
Homma himself took the stand.

369
00:14:53,232 --> 00:14:56,230
He told the court he had been removed in

370
00:14:56,250 --> 00:14:58,684
1942 because of his pro-British leanings

371
00:14:58,704 --> 00:15:00,377
and his disagreements with Tojo.

372
00:15:00,397 --> 00:15:02,071
He had trained in England,

373
00:15:02,091 --> 00:15:05,119
he said, and his time with the British had

374
00:15:05,139 --> 00:15:07,510
given him a broad outlook on life.

375
00:15:08,825 --> 00:15:11,876
He was asked about the bodies along the road.

376
00:15:11,896 --> 00:15:14,265
He had driven that road, he said,

377
00:15:14,285 --> 00:15:15,289
and seen none.

378
00:15:15,309 --> 00:15:17,678
"However," he added, "I was not looking

379
00:15:17,698 --> 00:15:19,745
for them particularly, to find them."

380
00:15:20,925 --> 00:15:23,299
The next day the chief prosecutor,

381
00:15:23,319 --> 00:15:25,294
Colonel Frank Meek, took over.

382
00:15:25,314 --> 00:15:27,688
You knew you had seventy thousand

383
00:15:27,708 --> 00:15:30,880
prisoners of war at the fall of Bataan?

384
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:31,279
Yes.

385
00:15:31,299 --> 00:15:34,072
Did you inquire as to the physical

386
00:15:34,092 --> 00:15:35,668
condition of the prisoners?

387
00:15:35,688 --> 00:15:36,885
"I did not."

388
00:15:38,225 --> 00:15:41,148
Homma admitted that a general had come to

389
00:15:41,168 --> 00:15:44,366
tell him more than a hundred men a day

390
00:15:44,386 --> 00:15:45,796
were dying at O'Donnell.

391
00:15:45,816 --> 00:15:48,299
And he said a sentence the prosecution

392
00:15:48,319 --> 00:15:49,014
would remember.

393
00:15:49,034 --> 00:15:51,160
"I am morally responsible for whatever

394
00:15:51,180 --> 00:15:53,325
happened in anything under my command."

395
00:15:54,650 --> 00:15:56,966
On the morning of the seventh he finished.

396
00:15:56,986 --> 00:15:59,886
"I came to know for the first time in the

397
00:15:59,906 --> 00:16:01,638
court of such atrocities," he said,

398
00:16:01,658 --> 00:16:03,390
"and I am ashamed of myself,

399
00:16:03,410 --> 00:16:04,870
should these atrocities have happened."

400
00:16:06,550 --> 00:16:09,190
That afternoon the defense called its last

401
00:16:09,210 --> 00:16:09,570
witness.

402
00:16:09,590 --> 00:16:12,790
Fujiko Homma, through two interpreters,

403
00:16:12,810 --> 00:16:15,077
told the commission she had married the

404
00:16:15,097 --> 00:16:17,650
general in 1926.

405
00:16:17,670 --> 00:16:20,500
He had disagreed with this war from the

406
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:21,568
beginning, she said.

407
00:16:21,588 --> 00:16:24,061
Japan had politicians, he used to say,

408
00:16:24,081 --> 00:16:25,150
but no statesmen.

409
00:16:26,425 --> 00:16:28,515
Military force, she said he believed,

410
00:16:28,535 --> 00:16:31,327
should be used to defend the homeland and

411
00:16:31,347 --> 00:16:34,140
keep the peace, and never to invade other

412
00:16:34,160 --> 00:16:34,492
countries.

413
00:16:34,512 --> 00:16:36,250
He liked reading, and poetry,

414
00:16:36,270 --> 00:16:38,359
and tennis under the blue sky.

415
00:16:38,379 --> 00:16:40,469
He did not hunt or fish.

416
00:16:40,489 --> 00:16:43,282
Then she said the line that went around

417
00:16:43,302 --> 00:16:44,102
the world.

418
00:16:45,275 --> 00:16:46,968
"I have come from Tokyo to here,

419
00:16:46,988 --> 00:16:49,661
and I am proud of the fact that I am the

420
00:16:49,681 --> 00:16:51,755
wife of General Homma.

421
00:16:51,775 --> 00:16:53,788
I have one daughter, and my wish for her

422
00:16:53,808 --> 00:16:55,820
is that some day I wish that she will

423
00:16:55,840 --> 00:16:57,175
marry a man like my husband,

424
00:16:57,195 --> 00:16:59,175
Masaharu Homma."

425
00:16:59,875 --> 00:17:02,198
Lieutenant Pelz wrote in his diary that

426
00:17:02,218 --> 00:17:04,542
the general sobbed as she said it,

427
00:17:04,562 --> 00:17:07,889
and that all of them had tears in their eyes.

428
00:17:07,909 --> 00:17:08,894
Colonel Meek said:

429
00:17:08,914 --> 00:17:10,902
"The Prosecution has no questions." Major

430
00:17:10,922 --> 00:17:12,911
Skeen announced that, with this witness,

431
00:17:12,931 --> 00:17:13,935
the defense rested.

432
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:17,369
A few minutes later the prosecution read

433
00:17:17,389 --> 00:17:19,245
the sworn statement of General King,

434
00:17:19,265 --> 00:17:21,121
taken in Washington three weeks before.

435
00:17:21,141 --> 00:17:23,935
In it King said of the man on trial:

436
00:17:23,955 --> 00:17:26,437
"I have never seen him in my life,

437
00:17:26,457 --> 00:17:28,020
so far as I know."

438
00:17:29,275 --> 00:17:31,449
If stories like this one matter to you,

439
00:17:31,469 --> 00:17:34,209
take a moment to subscribe to WWII Frontlines.

440
00:17:34,229 --> 00:17:37,205
We tell prisoner of war stories from the

441
00:17:37,225 --> 00:17:39,826
documents, and the next one is already

442
00:17:39,846 --> 00:17:40,646
being prepared.

443
00:17:42,375 --> 00:17:44,857
The closing arguments were made on the

444
00:17:44,877 --> 00:17:45,930
ninth of February.

445
00:17:45,950 --> 00:17:48,432
Major Skeen told the commission that the

446
00:17:48,452 --> 00:17:50,219
whole case was an indictment,

447
00:17:50,239 --> 00:17:53,079
not of an individual, but of the system

448
00:17:53,099 --> 00:17:55,223
and background of the Japanese Army.

449
00:17:55,243 --> 00:17:57,726
Lieutenant Pelz said the commander of the

450
00:17:57,746 --> 00:18:00,228
Fourteenth Army was damned if he did,

451
00:18:00,248 --> 00:18:02,015
and damned if he didn't.

452
00:18:02,035 --> 00:18:03,818
Homma, in civilian clothes,

453
00:18:03,838 --> 00:18:05,775
wept openly as Skeen spoke.

454
00:18:07,075 --> 00:18:08,432
Then Colonel Meek answered.

455
00:18:08,452 --> 00:18:11,186
Never would there be a blot in human

456
00:18:11,206 --> 00:18:13,595
history, he said, to compare with the

457
00:18:13,615 --> 00:18:14,283
Death March.

458
00:18:14,303 --> 00:18:16,693
"Someone is to blame for these marches,

459
00:18:16,713 --> 00:18:19,447
and that someone is the Accused in this

460
00:18:19,467 --> 00:18:20,267
court today."

461
00:18:21,450 --> 00:18:23,570
Meek reminded the court that Homma's

462
00:18:23,590 --> 00:18:25,839
headquarters stood a short walk from the

463
00:18:25,859 --> 00:18:27,784
road while seventy thousand Americans and

464
00:18:27,804 --> 00:18:29,405
Filipinos dragged themselves past it,

465
00:18:29,425 --> 00:18:30,377
day after day.

466
00:18:30,397 --> 00:18:32,322
If he had cared to listen,

467
00:18:32,342 --> 00:18:34,915
Meek said, he could have heard the screams

468
00:18:34,935 --> 00:18:36,860
of the wounded and the dying.

469
00:18:36,880 --> 00:18:40,102
And water, he said, is as free as the air

470
00:18:40,122 --> 00:18:40,922
you breathe.

471
00:18:42,525 --> 00:18:43,705
On the eleventh of February,

472
00:18:43,725 --> 00:18:46,525
1946, at three in the afternoon,

473
00:18:46,545 --> 00:18:49,225
Homma stood before the five generals with

474
00:18:49,245 --> 00:18:51,265
his senior counsel and his interpreter.

475
00:18:51,285 --> 00:18:53,645
The record shows that the commission found

476
00:18:53,665 --> 00:18:56,365
him guilty of the charge, and not guilty

477
00:18:56,385 --> 00:18:59,085
of the extra charge that his men had

478
00:18:59,105 --> 00:19:01,475
refused quarter on Corregidor.

479
00:19:02,175 --> 00:19:04,033
Homma was allowed to speak.

480
00:19:04,053 --> 00:19:06,551
He thanked the gentlemen of the commission

481
00:19:06,571 --> 00:19:09,427
for the courtesy with which he had been

482
00:19:09,447 --> 00:19:11,225
treated all through his trial.

483
00:19:11,245 --> 00:19:13,383
Then General Donovan read the sentence:

484
00:19:13,403 --> 00:19:15,900
to be shot to death with musketry.

485
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:18,777
The record notes that at five minutes past

486
00:19:18,797 --> 00:19:20,595
three the trial was concluded.

487
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,155
A firing squad, not the rope.

488
00:19:24,175 --> 00:19:26,693
One of his lawyers, Captain Frank Coder,

489
00:19:26,713 --> 00:19:29,231
told reporters that death before a firing

490
00:19:29,251 --> 00:19:31,044
squad is an honorable death.

491
00:19:31,064 --> 00:19:33,582
Years later one of the five judges,

492
00:19:33,602 --> 00:19:36,120
General Trudeau, wrote that he had thought

493
00:19:36,140 --> 00:19:37,484
Homma an outstanding soldier,

494
00:19:37,504 --> 00:19:40,556
and that he would not vote to hang him.

495
00:19:40,576 --> 00:19:41,921
That is his memory.

496
00:19:41,941 --> 00:19:44,652
The trial record says only that two thirds

497
00:19:44,672 --> 00:19:46,720
or more of the members agreed.

498
00:19:48,100 --> 00:19:50,858
Major Tisdelle was in the courtroom that

499
00:19:50,878 --> 00:19:51,226
afternoon.

500
00:19:51,246 --> 00:19:52,696
He told a reporter:

501
00:19:52,716 --> 00:19:54,534
"There are twenty-four hundred Americans

502
00:19:54,554 --> 00:19:56,739
and twenty-nine thousand Filipinos in this

503
00:19:56,759 --> 00:19:59,700
court room, too, but you can't see them."

504
00:20:01,300 --> 00:20:03,462
On the very same day, in Washington,

505
00:20:03,482 --> 00:20:05,645
the Supreme Court of the United States

506
00:20:05,665 --> 00:20:07,479
refused to hear Homma's appeal,

507
00:20:07,499 --> 00:20:08,913
six votes to two.

508
00:20:08,933 --> 00:20:11,423
Two justices wrote that it should have

509
00:20:11,443 --> 00:20:12,243
been heard.

510
00:20:13,400 --> 00:20:14,677
Justice Frank Murphy wrote:

511
00:20:14,697 --> 00:20:17,596
"Either we conduct such a trial as this in

512
00:20:17,616 --> 00:20:19,866
the noble spirit and atmosphere of our

513
00:20:19,886 --> 00:20:21,811
Constitution, or we abandon all pretense

514
00:20:21,831 --> 00:20:24,081
to justice, let the ages slip away,

515
00:20:24,101 --> 00:20:26,351
and descend to the level of revengeful

516
00:20:26,371 --> 00:20:27,171
blood purges."

517
00:20:28,325 --> 00:20:30,481
Murphy did not say Homma was innocent.

518
00:20:30,501 --> 00:20:32,846
He wrote that the evidence against him

519
00:20:32,866 --> 00:20:35,887
might well be clearer than in the case of

520
00:20:35,907 --> 00:20:38,985
General Yamashita, tried in the same city

521
00:20:39,005 --> 00:20:40,585
a few weeks earlier.

522
00:20:40,605 --> 00:20:42,985
What troubled him was the method.

523
00:20:43,005 --> 00:20:46,385
"Today the lives of Yamashita and Homma,

524
00:20:46,405 --> 00:20:48,761
leaders of enemy forces vanquished in the

525
00:20:48,781 --> 00:20:51,136
field of battle, are taken without regard

526
00:20:51,156 --> 00:20:53,512
to due process of law," he wrote.

527
00:20:53,532 --> 00:20:55,209
"But tomorrow the precedent here

528
00:20:55,229 --> 00:20:57,265
established can be turned against others."

529
00:20:58,625 --> 00:21:01,030
Justice Wiley Rutledge pointed to the

530
00:21:01,050 --> 00:21:03,456
rules the commission had worked under,

531
00:21:03,476 --> 00:21:05,881
written for it by MacArthur's command.

532
00:21:05,901 --> 00:21:08,306
They allowed forced confessions to be

533
00:21:08,326 --> 00:21:09,519
received in evidence.

534
00:21:09,539 --> 00:21:11,540
In his judgment, he wrote,

535
00:21:11,560 --> 00:21:13,985
those provisions spoiled the entire proceeding.

536
00:21:15,750 --> 00:21:19,120
There was one man left who could stop the

537
00:21:19,140 --> 00:21:20,627
execution in the Pacific.

538
00:21:20,647 --> 00:21:23,263
The sentence had to be reviewed and

539
00:21:23,283 --> 00:21:24,770
confirmed by General MacArthur,

540
00:21:24,790 --> 00:21:27,050
now ruling occupied Japan from Tokyo.

541
00:21:28,450 --> 00:21:30,523
On the eleventh of March, 1946,

542
00:21:30,543 --> 00:21:33,904
Fujiko Homma was received in his office.

543
00:21:33,924 --> 00:21:35,538
MacArthur gave her forty minutes.

544
00:21:35,558 --> 00:21:37,172
According to the Associated Press,

545
00:21:37,192 --> 00:21:39,786
she told him in broken English that it

546
00:21:39,806 --> 00:21:43,054
would be a great loss to the world if her

547
00:21:43,074 --> 00:21:44,034
husband were executed.

548
00:21:44,054 --> 00:21:46,322
But she did not ask for mercy,

549
00:21:46,342 --> 00:21:48,609
she said, because her husband would be

550
00:21:48,629 --> 00:21:50,590
angry with her if she did.

551
00:21:51,900 --> 00:21:55,134
She asked him to look again at the record

552
00:21:55,154 --> 00:21:56,219
of the trial.

553
00:21:56,239 --> 00:21:58,750
Captain George Furness, one of the defense

554
00:21:58,770 --> 00:22:00,196
lawyers, came with her.

555
00:22:00,216 --> 00:22:03,812
He told reporters it was not a plea for mercy.

556
00:22:03,832 --> 00:22:05,981
MacArthur, he said, promised to consider

557
00:22:06,001 --> 00:22:08,151
the record carefully, and said he

558
00:22:08,171 --> 00:22:10,340
understood and sympathized with her position.

559
00:22:11,700 --> 00:22:13,619
MacArthur's own statement to the press

560
00:22:13,639 --> 00:22:16,204
called the meeting one of the most trying

561
00:22:16,224 --> 00:22:17,496
moments of his life.

562
00:22:17,516 --> 00:22:19,758
No incident, he said, could more deeply

563
00:22:19,778 --> 00:22:22,343
illustrate the utter evil of war and its

564
00:22:22,363 --> 00:22:24,281
dreadful consequences upon those like her,

565
00:22:24,301 --> 00:22:26,240
who had no part in it.

566
00:22:27,775 --> 00:22:30,411
Ten days later, on the twenty-first of

567
00:22:30,431 --> 00:22:33,067
March, MacArthur's review was released in Tokyo.

568
00:22:33,087 --> 00:22:33,826
It began:

569
00:22:33,846 --> 00:22:36,483
"I am again confronted with the repugnant

570
00:22:36,503 --> 00:22:39,518
duty of passing final judgment on a former

571
00:22:39,538 --> 00:22:41,815
adversary in a major military campaign."

572
00:22:43,175 --> 00:22:44,855
The proceedings showed, he wrote,

573
00:22:44,875 --> 00:22:47,100
that Homma lacked the basic firmness of

574
00:22:47,120 --> 00:22:49,098
character and moral fortitude needed by

575
00:22:49,118 --> 00:22:50,431
officers in high command.

576
00:22:50,451 --> 00:22:53,095
Of the men who had surrendered on Bataan

577
00:22:53,115 --> 00:22:53,761
he wrote:

578
00:22:53,781 --> 00:22:56,092
"Of all fighting men of all time,

579
00:22:56,112 --> 00:22:58,757
none deserved more the honors of war in

580
00:22:58,777 --> 00:23:00,775
their final hour of final agony."

581
00:23:02,075 --> 00:23:05,048
Then he set down the doctrine that would

582
00:23:05,068 --> 00:23:06,170
outlive them all.

583
00:23:06,190 --> 00:23:08,415
"Soldiers of an army invariably reflect

584
00:23:08,435 --> 00:23:10,286
the attitude of their general.

585
00:23:10,306 --> 00:23:12,530
The leader is the essence." Isolated

586
00:23:12,550 --> 00:23:14,027
crimes might be exceptions,

587
00:23:14,047 --> 00:23:16,271
he wrote, but widespread and continuing

588
00:23:16,291 --> 00:23:18,890
abuse could only be the responsibility of

589
00:23:18,910 --> 00:23:21,155
the highest authority in the field.

590
00:23:22,475 --> 00:23:24,795
He answered the two justices too.

591
00:23:24,815 --> 00:23:27,915
No trial, he wrote, could have been fairer

592
00:23:27,935 --> 00:23:29,085
than this one.

593
00:23:29,105 --> 00:23:32,205
And then the sentence that closed the case.

594
00:23:32,225 --> 00:23:34,935
"If this defendant does not deserve his

595
00:23:34,955 --> 00:23:36,885
judicial fate, none in jurisdictional

596
00:23:36,905 --> 00:23:38,075
history ever did."

597
00:23:39,400 --> 00:23:41,660
MacArthur confirmed the sentence.

598
00:23:41,680 --> 00:23:45,611
Homma would keep his uniform and die by a

599
00:23:45,631 --> 00:23:47,305
firing squad, a soldier's death,

600
00:23:47,325 --> 00:23:49,338
as the Associated Press put it.

601
00:23:49,358 --> 00:23:51,710
In Tokyo the chief cabinet secretary said

602
00:23:51,730 --> 00:23:54,082
the decision taught the Japanese a grave

603
00:23:54,102 --> 00:23:54,421
lesson:

604
00:23:54,441 --> 00:23:56,793
a commanding general must answer for the

605
00:23:56,813 --> 00:23:59,842
acts of his men, even when he gives no

606
00:23:59,862 --> 00:24:00,662
order himself.

607
00:24:01,900 --> 00:24:04,096
Homma's daughter Hisako, eighteen years

608
00:24:04,116 --> 00:24:06,495
old, spoke to reporters for her mother,

609
00:24:06,515 --> 00:24:08,209
who was ill in bed.

610
00:24:08,229 --> 00:24:09,580
"Today's verdict," she said,

611
00:24:09,600 --> 00:24:12,000
"is of human making, not of God's."

612
00:24:13,775 --> 00:24:15,969
Homma had already written to his lawyers,

613
00:24:15,989 --> 00:24:17,724
on the eve of the verdict,

614
00:24:17,744 --> 00:24:20,063
that they had left no stone unturned to

615
00:24:20,083 --> 00:24:21,817
give him a fair trial, which,

616
00:24:21,837 --> 00:24:24,449
he wrote, he knew was denied to him from

617
00:24:24,469 --> 00:24:25,034
the outset.

618
00:24:25,054 --> 00:24:27,081
And he wrote that he respected Americans

619
00:24:27,101 --> 00:24:28,855
all the more because of them.

620
00:24:30,125 --> 00:24:32,232
Through the Japanese government he sent

621
00:24:32,252 --> 00:24:33,650
one more message home.

622
00:24:33,670 --> 00:24:36,132
"I know I cannot escape the death

623
00:24:36,152 --> 00:24:37,195
sentence," he wrote.

624
00:24:37,215 --> 00:24:40,031
"I want to go, the earlier the better,

625
00:24:40,051 --> 00:24:42,512
to the place where Japanese soldiers fell

626
00:24:42,532 --> 00:24:44,305
and where American soldiers sleep."

627
00:24:45,625 --> 00:24:47,865
He was taken to Los Baños,

628
00:24:47,885 --> 00:24:50,830
south of Manila, where the Americans now

629
00:24:50,850 --> 00:24:52,947
held their condemned Japanese prisoners.

630
00:24:52,967 --> 00:24:55,725
About six weeks earlier, General Yamashita

631
00:24:55,745 --> 00:24:56,845
had been hanged there.

632
00:24:58,050 --> 00:24:59,877
Shortly before one in the morning on the

633
00:24:59,897 --> 00:25:03,204
third of April, 1946, Homma walked out

634
00:25:03,224 --> 00:25:05,227
between a double rank of guards,

635
00:25:05,247 --> 00:25:07,587
a chaplain beside him, his arms bound

636
00:25:07,607 --> 00:25:08,599
behind his back.

637
00:25:08,619 --> 00:25:10,284
An officer read the charge,

638
00:25:10,304 --> 00:25:11,970
the finding and the sentence.

639
00:25:11,990 --> 00:25:15,341
A black hood was put over his head and a

640
00:25:15,361 --> 00:25:17,364
round white target, four inches across,

641
00:25:17,384 --> 00:25:19,070
was pinned over his heart.

642
00:25:20,325 --> 00:25:22,712
He made no final statement.

643
00:25:22,732 --> 00:25:25,600
Twelve riflemen stood fifteen paces away.

644
00:25:25,620 --> 00:25:28,488
Some of the rifles held blanks.

645
00:25:28,508 --> 00:25:32,338
The officer in charge raised his right arm.

646
00:25:32,358 --> 00:25:35,313
The Associated Press wrote that Homma died

647
00:25:35,333 --> 00:25:37,641
calm and silent to the end.

648
00:25:37,661 --> 00:25:40,765
Not far away, an ambulance crew stood waiting.

649
00:25:42,525 --> 00:25:44,446
Homma was not the only one.

650
00:25:44,466 --> 00:25:46,520
The two officers who had planned the

651
00:25:46,540 --> 00:25:47,705
movement of the prisoners,

652
00:25:47,725 --> 00:25:51,365
Yoshitake Kawane and Kurataro Hirano,

653
00:25:51,385 --> 00:25:55,775
were tried later in Yokohama and hanged in 1949.

654
00:25:56,475 --> 00:25:58,900
Was it justice, or revenge?

655
00:25:58,920 --> 00:26:01,345
The argument has never stopped.

656
00:26:01,365 --> 00:26:02,812
MacArthur's biographer D.

657
00:26:02,832 --> 00:26:05,747
Clayton James called the trial a

658
00:26:05,767 --> 00:26:07,214
miscarriage of justice.

659
00:26:07,234 --> 00:26:10,148
The survivors had their own answer,

660
00:26:10,168 --> 00:26:13,142
the one Sergeant Baldassarre gave outside

661
00:26:13,162 --> 00:26:13,810
the courtroom:

662
00:26:13,830 --> 00:26:15,812
he never gave us no trial.

663
00:26:15,832 --> 00:26:18,482
Robert Pelz, the young lawyer who wept in

664
00:26:18,502 --> 00:26:20,818
that ballroom, said fifty years later that

665
00:26:20,838 --> 00:26:23,154
he could not say with certainty that

666
00:26:23,174 --> 00:26:24,175
injustice was done.

667
00:26:25,425 --> 00:26:28,056
What lasted was the idea at the heart of

668
00:26:28,076 --> 00:26:30,413
the case, the one MacArthur wrote down and

669
00:26:30,433 --> 00:26:31,591
Japan's own government repeated:

670
00:26:31,611 --> 00:26:33,948
that a general answers for what his army

671
00:26:33,968 --> 00:26:36,325
does, whether or not he gives the order.

672
00:26:37,625 --> 00:26:41,339
In 1973 Homma's daughter put up a small

673
00:26:41,359 --> 00:26:43,131
memorial where her father was shot,

674
00:26:43,151 --> 00:26:45,819
and she wrote to one of his American lawyers.

675
00:26:45,839 --> 00:26:47,910
In his last letter, she told him,

676
00:26:47,930 --> 00:26:49,105
her father had written:

677
00:26:49,125 --> 00:26:51,920
Hisako, never forget there were the

678
00:26:51,940 --> 00:26:53,256
honorable gentlemen, the lawyers who

679
00:26:53,276 --> 00:26:55,145
fought for me at the Manila trial.

680
00:26:56,875 --> 00:26:59,221
If your father, grandfather or uncle was

681
00:26:59,241 --> 00:27:02,192
on Bataan or Corregidor, walked that road,

682
00:27:02,212 --> 00:27:04,470
or came home from O'Donnell or Cabanatuan,

683
00:27:04,490 --> 00:27:07,725
tell us his name and his story in the comments.

684
00:27:07,745 --> 00:27:10,675
Those names are the reason we make these films.

685
00:27:12,450 --> 00:27:15,618
Every spring, in the desert of New Mexico,

686
00:27:15,638 --> 00:27:18,010
soldiers and civilians walk across the

687
00:27:18,030 --> 00:27:20,799
White Sands Missile Range in the Bataan

688
00:27:20,819 --> 00:27:21,995
Memorial Death March.

689
00:27:22,015 --> 00:27:25,183
In March 2026 they walked it for the

690
00:27:25,203 --> 00:27:25,981
thirty-seventh time.

691
00:27:26,001 --> 00:27:28,770
New Mexico remembers Bataan because of the

692
00:27:28,790 --> 00:27:30,365
two hundredth Coast Artillery,

693
00:27:30,385 --> 00:27:31,959
its National Guard regiment,

694
00:27:31,979 --> 00:27:35,950
about eighteen hundred men strong in 1941.

695
00:27:35,970 --> 00:27:37,805
The Army says eight hundred and

696
00:27:37,825 --> 00:27:39,990
twenty-nine of them died or went missing.

697
00:27:41,375 --> 00:27:42,626
The trial record survives,

698
00:27:42,646 --> 00:27:44,531
all three thousand pages of it,

699
00:27:44,551 --> 00:27:47,708
and in it the words of the men who came

700
00:27:47,728 --> 00:27:50,249
back from that road to tell five generals

701
00:27:50,269 --> 00:27:51,520
what had happened there.

702
00:27:51,540 --> 00:27:54,472
As Major Tisdelle said on the day of the

703
00:27:54,492 --> 00:27:56,329
sentence, there were thousands more in

704
00:27:56,349 --> 00:27:56,948
that courtroom.

705
00:27:56,968 --> 00:27:58,515
You just couldn't see them.

